\[
\begin{gathered}
\mu=-\chi=\left(\frac{\partial E}{\partial N}\right) \approx-\frac{1}{2}(\mathrm{IP}+\mathrm{EA}) \\
\eta=\left(\frac{\partial^{2} E}{\partial N^{2}}\right) \approx-\frac{1}{2}(\mathrm{IP}-\mathrm{EA})
\end{gathered}
\]

The Fukui function \(f(r)\) is defined as a derivative of the electron density on the total number of electrons in the system. These global and condensed-to-atom local indexes were successfully applied to a variety of problems in chemical reactivity \({ }^{57,58}\). Using finite-difference approximation and condensed to atoms representation, Fukui functions for electrophilic ( \(f_{a}^{-}\)), nucleophilic
\(\left(f_{a}^{+}\right)\), and radical \(\left(f_{a}^{0}\right)\) reactions are defined as:
\[
f_{a}^{-}=q_{C}-q_{N} ; f_{a}^{+}=q_{N}-q_{A} ; f_{a}^{ \pm}=\frac{1}{2}\left(q_{C}+q_{A}\right)
\]

Another useful c-DFT reactivity descriptor is the electrophilicity index given by
\[
\omega=\mu^{2} / 2 \eta
\]
as well as it's condensed to atoms variants for electrophilic ( \(\omega_{a}^{-}\)), nucleophilic ( \(\omega_{a}^{+}\)) and radical ( \(\omega_{a}^{ \pm}\)) attacks: \({ }^{59}\)
\[
\omega_{a}^{-}=\omega f_{a}^{-} ; \omega_{a}^{+}=\omega f_{a}^{+} ; \omega_{a}^{ \pm}=\omega f_{a}^{ \pm}
\]

On the basis of the predicted with AIMNet-NSE vertical IPs, EAs, and charges, we could directly compute all listed c-DFT indexes. Fig. 4 displays the correlation plots for all nine quantities. The AIMNet-NSE model achieves an excellent quality of

